a7da3ee40a5" 13194139534227 17592186045417 true] [17592186414920 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-i-j$ and $b=-2\\,i.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t[-2\\,i-(-i-j)] \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t(-i+j) \\\\  & =(-1-t)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1-t,\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1+t+1)^{2}\\,i-(t-1)(-1-t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-3{t}^{2}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =(-{t}^{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)-(-0-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534227 17592186045417 true] [17592186414921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414906 13194139534227 17592186045417 true] [17592186414921 1069 "q-125042" 13194139534227 17592186045417 true] [17592186414921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534227 17592186045417 true] [17592186414921 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,